1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,730
Same robot.

2
00:00:00,730 --> 00:00:01,394
Same task.

3
00:00:01,394 --> 00:00:03,253
One run succeeds, one fails.

4
00:00:03,253 --> 00:00:07,103
Move the start three centimeters, and it fails every time.

5
00:00:07,103 --> 00:00:09,160
Our review found that weakness.

6
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:18,719
We fixed it with fifty new demonstrations, and re-tested it on a sealed schedule: twelve for twelve, where the old policy went three for twelve.

7
00:00:18,719 --> 00:00:22,569
But at the trained spot, the evidence is still not enough.

8
00:00:22,569 --> 00:00:25,025
So QuantGate does not approve it yet.

9
00:00:26,025 --> 00:00:33,879
For quantization, we need to pick the candidate that has earned its next test, not an approval.

10
00:00:33,879 --> 00:00:37,600
That's why robot researchers need Quant Gate.

11
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:45,800
This is an S O one oh one arm running an A C T policy, Action Chunking with Transformers, on a Jetson Orin Nano Super.

12
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:52,629
We taught it with one hundred and twenty teleoperated demonstrations, and trained it with Nebius Jobs.

13
00:00:52,629 --> 00:01:05,015
From that one policy, we built twenty-six candidate engines with TensorRT: full precision, half precision, and twenty-four int eight builds that differ only in how they were calibrated.

14
00:01:05,015 --> 00:01:08,564
Every trial runs from a sealed, interleaved schedule.

15
00:01:08,564 --> 00:01:15,125
The operator sees only trial numbers, and the results stay sealed until all the trials are logged.

16
00:01:15,425 --> 00:01:17,766
First, measure on the real device.

17
00:01:17,766 --> 00:01:24,719
On this Jetson, half precision was the fastest engine, and the cheapest per inference, not int eight.

18
00:01:24,719 --> 00:01:30,089
On the arm, every candidate succeeded ninety to a hundred percent of the time.

19
00:01:30,089 --> 00:01:34,633
And the offline audit's top picks did no better than random picks.

20
00:01:34,633 --> 00:01:37,525
Twenty-five trials cannot tell them apart.

21
00:01:37,825 --> 00:01:42,384
What moved success was the deployment condition, not the precision.

22
00:01:42,384 --> 00:01:45,651
A new can worked forty-eight out of forty-eight.

23
00:01:45,651 --> 00:01:50,210
Without the gripper pads, success fell to eight out of twenty-four.

24
00:01:50,210 --> 00:01:57,900
So the fix was data: fifty demonstrations at shifted positions, fine-tuned in twenty-five minutes on Nebius Jobs.

25
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:00,419
The A I layers get checked too.

26
00:02:00,419 --> 00:02:07,146
In its first setup, Cosmos Reason, on a Nebius A I Endpoint, caught one failure in twenty-one.

27
00:02:07,146 --> 00:02:15,449
We tested it like any other component, on held-out recordings, and a validated question caught twenty of twenty-one.

28
00:02:15,449 --> 00:02:23,250
Nemotron Super on Token Factory explains the review in words, while every verdict and number comes from code.

29
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:26,071
So QuantGate answers three questions.

30
00:02:26,071 --> 00:02:28,524
Which candidate do I validate first?

31
00:02:28,524 --> 00:02:29,546
Half precision.

32
00:02:29,546 --> 00:02:30,909
Which do I distrust?

33
00:02:30,909 --> 00:02:38,882
Broken calibrations, which a simple input check catches, and any engine whose files no longer match what was audited.

34
00:02:38,882 --> 00:02:40,585
Can I approve deployment?

35
00:02:40,585 --> 00:02:41,131
Not yet.

36
00:02:41,131 --> 00:02:46,650
The review lists exactly which trials, and which rule changes, are still missing.

37
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:47,775
Quant Gate.

38
00:02:47,775 --> 00:02:50,550
Earn the next test, not the approval.
